"MHLA: Restoring Expressivity of Linear Attention via Token-Level Multi-Head (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07832v1)\nciteturn26854view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07832v1\",\"lineno\":192}); Total lines: 616\nL188: We empirically validate this behavior in Fig. cite81†3b , where MHLA consistently achieves a substantially higher attention-score rank than other linear attention variants— and does so without relying on auxiliary components such as depth-wise convolutions. This confirms that MHLA natively restores much of the representational capacity lost in global linear attention, whose rank remains strictly limited by $d$ regardless of the sequence length $N$.\nL189: #### Sparsity analysis.\nL190: The learned coefficient matrix $\\mathcal{M}_{c}$ allows each query-block to assign higher weights to a subset of blocks that are more relevant, effectively pruning irrelevant tokens at the block level. Within each selected block, the kernel inner products $\\widetilde{q}^{\\top}\\widetilde{K}_{t}$ further differentiate token contributions, leading to sharper and more concentrated attention distributions. We validate this effect empirically in Fig.\nL191: cite81†3b , where MHLA consistently yields lower attention entropy compared to other linear-attention baselines and even the softmax attention. This confirms that MHLA preserves query-conditioned selectivity and achieves substantially higher sparsity, enabling the model to attend to a small, semantically relevant subset of tokens rather than spreading attention uniformly.\nL192: Table 1: Comparison between Self Attention, Linear Attention, and MHLA. We report computation complexity, maximum achievable rank, memory complexity and query-conditioned selectivity.\nL193: \nL194: Method  | Time Complexity  | Rank Bound  | Memory Complexity  | Query-Conditioned\nL195: Self Attention  | $O(N^{2}d)$  | $N$  | $O(N^{2})$  | ✓\nL196: Linear Attention  | $O(Nd^{2})$  | $d$  | $O(d^{2})$  | ✗\nL197: MHLA (ours)  | $O(Nd^{2}+M^{2}d^{2})$  | $\\sum_{b=1}^{M}\\!\\min(n_{b},d)$  | $O(Md^{2})$  | ✓\nL198: #### Efficiency analysis.\nL199: The computation of MHLA consists of local Key–value summary computation, Multi-Head Mixing, and output computation, with a time complexity of $O\\!\\left(MN_{b}d^{2}+M^{2}d^{2}+MN_{b}d^{2}\\right)=O(Nd^{2}+M^{2}d^{2}).$ To better capture local information while ensuring efficiency, the number of blocks $M$ is usually set to satisfy $M^{2}\\leq N$. Therefore, $Nd^{2}$ becomes the leading term and the time complexity of MHLA is $O(Nd^{2})$.\nL200: The comparison of self attention, linear attention, and MHLA is summarized in Tab. cite98†1 . We also provide an empirical analysis of the scaling relationship between N and M in Appendix cite56†F.4 that verifies the induced complexity.\nL201: ## 5 Experiments\nL202: \nL203: Table 2: Comparison on Image Classification task. MHLA achieves the best accuracy with minimal parameter overhead on DeiT models, and outperforms Transformer-, LA-, and Mamba-based SOTAs. Results marked with an * are reproduced under the same training setup as MHLA-VLT.\nL204: \nL205: (a) Comparison of different attentions on DeiT.\nL206: Attention Type  | Params  | FLOPs  | Top1-ACC\nL207: Comparison on Deit-T Setting\nL208: Self Attn  | 5.7M  | 1.1G  | 72.2\nL209: Linear Attn  | 5.7M  | 1.1G  | 69.8\nL210: Focused LA [cite77†24 ]  | 6.1M  | 1.1G  | 74.1\nL211: Inline Attn [cite78†25 ]  | 6.5M  | 1.1G  | 74.5\nL212: MALA [cite99†20 ]  | 6.3M  | 1.1G  | 75.1\nL213: MHLA (Ours)  | 5.7M  | 1.1G  | 75.8\nL214: Comparison on Deit-S Setting\nL215: Self Attn  | 22M  | 4.2G  | 79.8\nL216: Linear Attn  | 22M  | 4.2G  | 77.6\nL217: RALA [cite76†21 ]  | 24M  | 4.6G  | 80.4\nL218: MALA [cite99†20 ]  | 24M  | 4.6G  | 80.3\nL219: MHLA (Ours)  | 22M  | 4.2G  | 81.0\nL220: (b) Comparison with SOTA models on ImageNet-1K.\nL221: Cost  | Model  | Params  | FLOPs  | Top1-ACC\nL222: $\\sim$2.5G  | FL-PVT-T [cite77†24 ]  | 12M  | 2.0G  | 77.8\nL223: FL-PVTv2-B1 [cite77†24 ]  | 13M  | 2.2G  | 79.5\nL224: MSVMamba-M [cite100†45 ]  | 12M  | 1.5G  | 79.8\nL225: NAT-M [cite101†26 ]  | 20M  | 2.7G  | 81.8\nL226: RAVLT-T [cite76†21 ]  | 15M  | 2.4G  | 82.3^{∗}\nL227: MAViT-T [cite99†20 ]  | 16M  | 2.5G  | 82.4^{∗}\nL228: MHLA-VLT-T  | 16M  | 2.4G  | 82.6\nL229: $\\sim$4.5G  | FAT-B3 [cite102†22 ]  | 29M  | 4.4G  | 83.6\nL230: Vmamba-T [cite103†32 ]  | 30M  | 4.9G  | 82.6\nL231: MV-T [cite104†27 ]  | 32M  | 4.4G  | 82.3\nL232: MSVMamba-T [cite100†45 ]  | 32M  | 5.1G  | 83.0\nL233: MAViT-S [cite99†20 ]  | 27M  | 4.6G  | 84.3^{∗}\nL234: MHLA-VLT-S  | 27M  | 4.6G  | 84.6\nL235: ### 5.1 Image Classification\nL236: #### Settings.\nL237: We adopt the training configurations from prior work [cite76†21 , cite99†20 , cite105†47 ]. The proposed MHLA is integrated into two representative architectures, DeiT [cite105†47 ] and VLT [cite76†21 ], across multiple model scales. The models are trained on ImageNet-1K [cite106†14 ]. For VLT, we strictly follow the setup in [cite76†21 ]. All models are trained for 300 epochs with a batch size of 1024 and a peak learning rate of 1e-3.\nL238: For models with an input size of 224, we pad the input size to 256 for better splitting of heads. The head number $M$ is set to 16 if there is no extra description. See Appendix cite50†E for more details.\nL239: #### Results.\nL240: We evaluate the pretrained DeiT models described above and report the result in Tab. cite107†2a , which clearly shows the superior performance of the proposed MHLA. We reach the best accuracy in linear attention across all model sizes, while introducing the fewest extra parameters compared with baselines. We then port the proposed MHLA to VLT [cite76†21 ] and evaluate the performance under the same settings. The results are shown in Tab.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMHLA: Restoring Expressivity of Linear Attention via Token-Level Multi-Head (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07832v1)\nciteturn26854view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07832v1\",\"lineno\":295}); Total lines: 616\nL289: Video generation involves extremely long sequence lengths, where quadratic attention becomes prohibitively slow. To evaluate MHLA under such ultra-long contexts, we fine-tune a pretrained Wan2.1-1.3B model by replacing its FlashAttention modules with MHLA. For comparison, we also fine-tune a version where all attention layers are replaced with vanilla linear attention (LA).\nL290: The training uses 81-frame videos at 480$\\times$800 resolution, corresponding to a sequence length of 31,500 tokens, with the mixing-head number $M=105$. In addition, we train a hybrid model where only 2/3 of the layers are replaced by MHLA.\nL291: We evaluate all models on VBench, and the results are reported in Tab. cite30†5.3 . MHLA delivers substantially stronger performance than vanilla LA while maintaining the same latency. At this extreme sequence length, vanilla LA suffers severe degradation due to global context collapse, whereas MHLA preserves linear-time complexity and recovers performance comparable to the original FlashAttention-based Wan2.1-1.3B, achieving a 2.1$\\times$ inference speedup.\nL292: The hybrid model provides an excellent trade-off, achieving a 1.6$\\times$ speedup with even better overall performance.\nL293: We further visualize the training loss curves in Fig. cite118†6 . MHLA rapidly adapts during fine-tuning and quickly approaches the pretrained model’s loss trajectory. In contrast, vanilla LA effectively fails to train under such long sequences, with its loss plateauing at a high level. This validates our analysis of global context collapse and demonstrates that conventional linear attention breaks down entirely in ultra-long visual sequence settings.\nL294: Model  | FID$\\downarrow$  | CLIP $\\uparrow$  | GenEval $\\uparrow$\nL295: PixArt-$\\alpha$ [cite116†7 ]  | 6.14  | 27.55  | 0.48\nL296: PixArt-$\\Sigma$ [cite119†6 ]  | 6.34  | 27.62  | 0.52\nL297: SANA* [cite113†52 ]  | 6.10  | 28.15  | 0.64\nL298: SANA-MHLA  | 5.90  | 28.26  | 0.68\nL299: \nL300: Table 4: Comparison on T2I models.\nL301: \nL302: Figure 5: Loss comparison.\nL303: Model  | Quality $\\uparrow$  | Semantic $\\uparrow$  | Total $\\uparrow$  | Latency (s) $\\downarrow$\nL304: Wan-FA  | 85.23  | 75.65  | 83.31  | 166\nL305: Wan-LA  | 69.96  | 11.38  | 58.24  | 82\nL306: Wan-MHLA  | 84.26  | 76.16  | 82.62  | 81\nL307: Wan-MHLA-H  | 84.87  | 79.59  | 83.82  | 103\nL308: \nL309: Table 5: MHLA in Video Generation. Wan-FA indicates a pretrained Wan2.1-1.3B. Wan-MHLA and Wan-LA replace all layers with MHLA and Linear Attention, respectively. Wan-MHLA-H only replaces 2/3 layers.\nL310: Figure 6: Loss comparison on Wan-2.1-1.3B. MHLA shows a much stronger convergence capability.\nL311: ### 5.4 Natural Language Processing\nL312: \nL313: To evaluate MHLA under autoregressive modeling, we test its performance in language modeling. Following GLA [cite111†55 ], we train a 0.3B model from scratch on 10B tokens from FineWeb-Edu [cite120†35 ] with a batch size of 0.25M tokens, using a cosine learning rate schedule (max LR 3e-4), weight decay of $0.01$, and gradient clipping of $1.0$. The head number $M$ is set to 32 for MHLA with a training context length of 2048.\nL314: #### Common-sense reasoning and MMLU.\nL315: \nL316: In Tab. cite121†6 , we present the language modeling perplexity, zero-shot accuracy on commonsense reasoning benchmarks, and MMLU. The proposed MHLA shows a comparable performance with Transformer++ [cite122†48 ] and the state-of-the-art linear models, including Gated DeltaNet (GDN) [cite123†54 ] and Mamba2 [cite124†11 ]. Additionally, MHLA outperforms all the baselines on the aggregated benchmark MMLU.\nL317: #### Long context understanding.\nL318: \nL319: As presented in Tab. cite125†8 , we evalute the models performance on LongBench [cite126†1 ]. The proposed MHLA shows explicit advantages over other SOTA recurrent models, especillly in Mulit-Doc QA, Summarization, and Code tasks, and achieves the highest average score. The result demonstrates the superior long context understanding capability of the proposed MHLA.\nL320: Table 6: MHLA in NLP. We report results evaluated on models trained with 10B tokens. We highlight the best and second best entries.\nL321: Model  | MMLU  | CSR  | Wino.  | PIQA  | ARC-c  | OBQA  | ARC-e  | BoolQ  | Wiki.  | LMB.\nL322:  | acc $\\uparrow$  | avg. $\\uparrow$  | acc $\\uparrow$  | acc $\\uparrow$  | acc_n $\\uparrow$  | acc_n $\\uparrow$  | acc_n $\\uparrow$  | acc $\\uparrow$  | ppl $\\downarrow$  | ppl $\\downarrow$\nL323: GLA (340M)  | 22.9  | 46.0  | 50.0  | 62.9  | 25.5  | 31.0  | 45.8  | 60.8  | 41.47  | 86.98\nL324: Transformer++(340M)  | 22.9  | 46.8  | 49.6  | 64.4  | 25.7  | 32.8  | 48.1  | 60.5  | 34.57  | 60.46\nL325: Mamba (390M)  | 23.5  | 46.4  | 50.5  | 64.1  | 24.9  | 32.4  | 48.3  | 58.2  | 38.32  | 62.43\nL326: Mamba2 (340M)  | 23.0  | 47.0  | 49.8  | 64.6  | 25.5  | 32.0  | 49.2  | 61.2  | 35.40  | 58.51\nL327: GDN (360M)  | 23.0  | 46.9  | 51.3  | 64.5  | 25.4  | 31.4  | 47.3  | 62.0  | 35.01  | 60.16\nL328: MHLA (340M)  | 23.7  | 47.1  | 51.3  | 64.4  | 25.9  | 33.4  | 46.5  | 61.3  | 38.31  | 71.64\nL329: ### 5.5 Ablation Study\nL330: \nL331: Table 7: Ablation study of the proposed MHLA.\nL332: \nL333: (a) Ablation of init strategy on DeiT-T. LB-init denotes Locality-biased Initialization.\nL334: \nL335: LB-init  | Learnable  | Top1-acc(%)\nL336:  | ✓  | 75.4\nL337: ✓  |  | 75.1\nL338: ✓  | ✓  | 75.8\nL339: \nL340: (b) Token-level head number ablation on DiT-S/2, 512px.\nL341: \nL342: Head number  | FID$\\downarrow$  | Throughput$\\uparrow$\nL343: 4  | 79.56  | 435\nL344: 16 